00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:48.088
And, but, but that location had one
important thing,

00:04:48.088 --> 00:04:53.328
it had Columbia University, where someone
explained to me what the Arpanet was.

00:04:53.328 --> 00:04:57.810
And that caused me to get a job at Sun
Microsystems.

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:00.402
Again, that crappy sales territory, and

00:05:00.402 --> 00:05:05.130
you could say I made my own break because
I asked some questions.

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:07.760
But that unlucky break led to a lucky
break.

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:11.692
Sun Microsystem employee number 50
something, I can't remember.

00:05:11.692 --> 00:05:13.998
And I really thought I was a big shot.

00:05:13.998 --> 00:05:18.710
I was 26, 27, selling you know, a boatload
of computer.

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:21.978
Got promoted, and then I met Vinod Costla.

00:05:21.978 --> 00:05:22.780
Holy Cow.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.029
This guy's as old as I am, he's a board
member.

00:05:25.029 --> 00:05:26.440
Scott McNeilio came here.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:27.440
Holy cow.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:30.864
This guy's, [UNKNOWN] I mean, he's the
president, the CEO.

00:05:30.864 --> 00:05:34.770
And then I learned the words venture
capitalist.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:37.375
I had no idea what that was, but it
sounded pretty good to me.

00:05:37.375 --> 00:05:40.626
And so, I just figured out what a venture
guy was, and

00:05:40.626 --> 00:05:42.878
I decided that I wanna be one of them.

00:05:42.878 --> 00:05:47.494
And I figured that I should get a masters,
learn a little more, need to get a second

00:05:47.494 --> 00:05:51.667
masters, and then I wrote 80 letters to
venture firms, letting them know,

00:05:51.667 --> 00:05:56.040
I'm gonna be in California, trying to
sweet talk all the assistants.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:00.927
I got an interview with Don Valentine at
five o'clock on a Monday,

00:06:00.927 --> 00:06:05.303
who took me to his office and asked me,
what's important?

00:06:05.303 --> 00:06:07.162
[LAUGH] Of course, I knew what's
important.

00:06:07.162 --> 00:06:09.066
I spoke for about seven, eight minutes.

00:06:09.066 --> 00:06:11.320
I gave it, I, I mean, I gave it all.

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:17.195
30 seconds of silence, and he said, what
else?

00:06:17.195 --> 00:06:20.300
[LAUGH]
I, I'd just given it all and

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:24.580
I said, Don, I gave you everything I know
I can't give you any more.

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:30.110
But the fact that I was so candid, and the
fact that I told it like it is, and

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:33.350
the fact that I had a sales background
caused me to be hired but

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:38.740
to make a long story short, it was a lucky
break to sell at Columbia.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:42.390
It was a lucky break to ask a few
questions, it was a lucky break to learn

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:45.480
about Sun Micro Systems where I wrote a, a
letter and

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:50.550
got a cold call interview and so in some
cases I think you make your own break.

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:52.330
But make no mistake.

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:55.420
A great deal of the reason as to why I'm
here has to do with luck.

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:58.540
I firmly believe that.

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:01.390
&gt;&gt; If you if, if you hadn't come back to
Silicon Valley,

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:04.900
do you think you'd still be in sales or
what, where, what was your vision?

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:10.280
&gt;&gt; So about six, seven years ago I was in
New York City.

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:13.750
And I got a call from someone, there's an
HP reunion in

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:18.644
New York City in some restaurant on 22nd
Street.

00:07:18.644 --> 00:07:25.410
And back in Hewlett-Packard, back in 79,
it was the Italian mafia.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:29.390
John Colucci, Tartaglia, I mean all these
guys,

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:31.365
the same names as The Godfather in some
ways.

00:07:31.365 --> 00:07:36.440
[LAUGH] And, and so I decided to attend
this gathering.

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:39.420
And it was one of those scenes from movies
that you

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:42.030
occasional see where your life would have
been.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:44.010
And let me tell you, it would not have
been a bad life.

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:49.290
I think everybody was a lifer, everybody
had a home, their kids were happy, but

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:53.720
they got up and started singing song the
HP way to Bill and Dave.

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:55.260
This is the two fantasy packet.

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:58.959
And they gave me a snippet of what life
might have been, and

00:07:58.959 --> 00:08:01.321
it completely freaked me out.

00:08:01.321 --> 00:08:05.030
And so that's what it could have been.

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:09.430
I doubt it would have been that, but that
was a very eye-opening ex, experience.

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:11.230
&gt;&gt; Interesting.

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:15.630
Well, I want to I wanna move now to what
people have no doubt come here to

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:19.575
hear about, which is your leadership of
Sequoia and the success you and

00:08:19.575 --> 00:08:23.816
your partners have had over not only
recently but over the last few decades.

00:08:23.816 --> 00:08:25.344
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:08:25.344 --> 00:08:27.809
&gt;&gt; it's, it's obviously been an incredible
year.

00:08:27.809 --> 00:08:31.410
And it's been an incredible decade, for a
few decades.

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:34.350
And I'm wondering what about Sequoia
separates it from the others,

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:36.280
that's allowed it to maintain success for
so long.

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:39.711
What's in the secret sauce as, as you've
put it, and, and

00:08:39.711 --> 00:08:41.880
how've you stayed on top for so long?

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:46.395
&gt;&gt; Well, I think it all started with Don
Valentine, who's the founder at

00:08:46.395 --> 00:08:51.360
Sequoia Capital who had two lessons that
were pivotal in my mind.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:55.370
One was really an appreciation for
markets, and

00:08:55.370 --> 00:09:00.833
second an ability to recruit
non-conventional people.

00:09:00.833 --> 00:09:06.980
So he recruited Mike Morads, whose name
you probably know, who was a writer.

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:14.180
Had written a book at Apple, was the San
Francisco Time Magazine bureau chief.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:16.320
And he recruited him to be an investor.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:19.530
Now you may wonder, how does, how do you
recruit him to be an investor?

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:24.220
Because he liked the way Mike thought, he
liked the way Mike asked questions.

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:29.620
Right after he recruited Mike, he, he
hired me.

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:36.610
And he hired me because of my view of a
business from the customer in,

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:38.800
versus from the product out.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:42.170
And Mike and I could not be more
different.

00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:48.480
Mike is a, is a thinking man of few words,
Brit.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:52.890
I'm an Italian, a few too many words and
so on.

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:56.430
But Mike and I learned to work together
for 20 something years, but

00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:58.750
it's to, to Don's credit.

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:00.320
And we kind of kept that going.

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:06.930
So we continue to hire people mostly from
modest means,

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:12.853
people that have not followed a preset set
of tracks.

00:10:12.853 --> 00:10:15.197
People that have taken risks,

00:10:15.197 --> 00:10:19.080
people that have achieved some kind of
success early on.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:24.140
People that are smart, but that goes
without saying.

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:26.990
People that have both IQ and EQ.

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:34.530
And then our spec has product management,
technical background,

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.600
has all these conventional lines.

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:42.020
And that has a line at the end that says,
doesn't meet spec, but he or

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:42.990
she is special.

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:48.720
And it's the last line that's the most
important line of the recruit.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.950
And so, we meet a lot of people, we're
always looking for people.

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:54.547
We're looking for people right now, both
in the venture business and

00:10:54.547 --> 00:10:56.340
in the growth business.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:58.810
We get to know them quite well.

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:02.380
And then we asked them to listen to
companies with us.

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:04.650
We just wanna see how they think.

00:11:04.650 --> 00:11:08.080
And we look for highly imperfect human
beings,

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:12.140
the type of people that were probably
individual contributors early in life.

00:11:12.140 --> 00:11:17.220
The quirky kids if you will, not the
quarterback, or the football team, or

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:19.890
you know, the female equivalent of that.

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:24.300
But people that have a bit of an edge,
people that have something to prove,

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:26.690
people that need to win, and

00:11:26.690 --> 00:11:31.740
people that are not asses when you peel a
few layers, because life is too short.

00:11:32.780 --> 00:11:39.000
And we ta, and our real secret is take
these heterogeneous individual

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:45.150
contributors, and showing them on how to
work as part of a team.

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:47.490
To start using the we pronoun.

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:51.800
Take full responsibilities for failure,
and share the successes.

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:56.530
And if there's one reason why we've lasted
for 43 years near the top or

00:11:56.530 --> 00:12:00.840
at the top of our business, is because of
this.

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:06.390
The, this leads to a high performance
culture that has only one goal.

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:09.900
A team-oriented spirit to help founders
build great companies.

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:15.910
&gt;&gt; So you brought up Mike Morris and you
two have led the firm for some time.

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:21.130
And he had a quote not too long ago where
he said about Sequoia that quote,

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:24.700
we're always outgunned by companies that
are far larger than us,

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:26.655
who have threatened us and the founders
with extinction.

00:12:26.655 --> 00:12:29.100
It's incredibly thrilling to prove
everyone wrong.

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:31.596
You can't get a bigger rush than that.

00:12:31.596 --> 00:12:35.380
Now you talked about your talent
recruitment and recruiting hungry people.

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:38.790
But there's gotta be more to it to keep
you hungry as a firm.

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:40.780
To keep you from resting you on your
laurels,

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:42.640
to keep you from resting on your laurels.

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:45.740
How do you think about continuing to
motivate yourself and motivating people?

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:48.147
What kind of Things do you implement with,
as a,

00:12:48.147 --> 00:12:50.811
as a firm wide culture to, to make sure
that happens?

00:12:50.811 --> 00:12:52.383
&gt;&gt; There's a few things.

00:12:52.383 --> 00:12:56.260
First of all, I think we're paranoid as
heck.

00:12:56.260 --> 00:13:01.750
We're always one or two new investments
alone from becoming a second tier firm.

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:05.530
And that is very clear in our minds.

00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:10.929
Second, we have a different view towards
risk, than most other people.

00:13:10.929 --> 00:13:14.260
People hate to do things because they view
them as risky.

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:21.100
We actually think that if you're a crystal
clear thinker, doing nothing is risky.

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:24.050
So we're always changing and implementing.

00:13:24.050 --> 00:13:25.960
And the old adage that if it ain't broke,

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:28.980
don't fix it does not apply to Sequoia
Capital.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:30.520
It is the exact opposite.

00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:33.670
If something is working like a dream,
break it,

00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:37.090
because you know there are some
competitors out there who are looking at

00:13:37.090 --> 00:13:40.290
you from the outside and are trying to
push you out of business.

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:42.200
So the last thing we've done in the last
four or

00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:44.840
five months, we've taken all the posters
down.

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:49.360
We had all these posters, 200 IPO's, 20%
of the NASDAQ blah, blah,

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:51.480
blah, take them all off the walls.

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:56.850
Take them all off the walls and let's act
as if we haven't had one single win.

00:13:56.850 --> 00:14:01.126
And so where we, screwing around, messing
with the formula, trying new things,

00:14:01.126 --> 00:14:07.390
we went into China, we went into India
because we asked ourselves a question.

00:14:07.390 --> 00:14:12.040
Where are the most valuable companies
gonna come from in the next 20 years?

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:13.010
It was no longer clear.

00:14:13.010 --> 00:14:13.950
They would come from the US.

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:18.390
So we went into India and China, and we
did in a decentralized fashion.

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:20.700
We met some people over a period of 90
days and

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:23.280
we told them, we'd like you to be Sequoia
Capital China.

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:26.170
You make all the decisions, not us.

00:14:26.170 --> 00:14:27.520
No other firm has done that.

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:32.300
So always taking risks because taking
risks is the only way to keep on going.

00:14:33.302 --> 00:14:37.500
&gt;&gt; I wanna get to your success in China a
little bit later, but first I

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:41.390
wanna ask something that I think a fair
number of my classmates are interested in,

00:14:41.390 --> 00:14:45.110
which is about how you pitch the VCs and
how you pitch people like Sequoia.

00:14:46.134 --> 00:14:50.556
The web is filled with stories of how
difficult it is to pitch you, and

00:14:50.556 --> 00:14:51.634
pitch Sequoia.

00:14:51.634 --> 00:14:54.030
And I was gonna share some stories, but I,

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:57.903
there are a lot of words that I can't
actually use on stage here.

00:14:57.903 --> 00:15:00.951
So I'll just say there, there's some,
there's some aggressive.

00:15:00.951 --> 00:15:04.370
It's an aggressive pitch made in usually.

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:07.280
And so I'm wondering what you look for in
pitches.

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:10.310
What what sets the best ones apart?

00:15:10.310 --> 00:15:13.250
And what tips might you have for people
that wanna come in and pitch you?

00:15:13.250 --> 00:15:15.760
&gt;&gt; So, let me set it up, up first.

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:19.880
We're very cognizant that when somebody
comes to Sequoia.

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:24.540
They prepared, we are very cognizant of
the fact that in many case, it's their

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:27.584
big day, one of many large days, maybe
they are going to [INAUDIBLE] and

00:15:27.584 --> 00:15:29.530
maybe they are going to bench mark, so we
did.

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:31.180
So we take that quite seriously.

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:35.180
The meetings at Sequoia start exactly on
time,

00:15:35.180 --> 00:15:41.180
they end on time and no one has their
iPhone on, it goes against.

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:45.050
All our culture, no ones doing any else,
just to be straight.

00:15:45.050 --> 00:15:49.870
Now, we also don't have flower children at
Sequoia Capital.

00:15:49.870 --> 00:15:51.650
We do ask questions.

00:15:51.650 --> 00:15:55.630
And, and I will tell you there are some
partnerships who are afraid of

00:15:55.630 --> 00:15:59.820
asking questions for ruining their
reputation.

00:15:59.820 --> 00:16:01.800
No we'll ask questions.

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:04.890
And we try to treat people the way we
would treat someone in our living room.

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:07.150
But if you have a question about the
market size,

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:09.620
if you have a question about some issues,
we're gonna ask it.

00:16:09.620 --> 00:16:11.460
And unfortunately, because we ask three or

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:15.160
four questions, once in a while, we have a
founder who we realizes.

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:17.890
Oh my god, I, I just started a company
that, that,

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:21.410
that's chasing a $42 million global
market.

00:16:21.410 --> 00:16:23.020
That does not feel good.

00:16:23.020 --> 00:16:27.370
But it's not meant to be rude, it's meant
to understand the person's business.

00:16:27.370 --> 00:16:29.932
So, some of the best pitches.

00:16:29.932 --> 00:16:33.830
Drew Houston who was gonna

00:16:33.830 --> 00:16:40.180
launch a company in a crowded market, and
yet could clearly articulate.

00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:43.580
Why none of the existing products were
gonna make it.

00:16:43.580 --> 00:16:48.280
Crystal clear thinking is one of the
things we look for.

00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:49.805
Not a fancy slide pitch.

00:16:49.805 --> 00:16:55.290
[COUGH] But crystal clear thinking and we
pay careful attention to little words.

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:56.320
To the pronoun being used.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:00.600
When someone says, I can ship you this
thing.

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:01.700
It's a little warning flag.

00:17:01.700 --> 00:17:02.790
What do you mean you can ship us?

00:17:02.790 --> 00:17:03.780
You're not shipping us anything.

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:05.100
It's your company that's shipping us.

00:17:05.100 --> 00:17:08.620
So we pay really careful attention to
everything.

00:17:08.620 --> 00:17:12.520
Or Fred Leddy who had a company that was
growing like a weed and

00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:16.540
all he told us was all the mistakes he
made.

00:17:16.540 --> 00:17:18.290
Those were the great pictures.

00:17:18.290 --> 00:17:20.970
Those were people that were self aware.

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:23.300
They were crystal clear thinkers.

00:17:23.300 --> 00:17:26.510
They were willing to learn, willing to
listen.

00:17:26.510 --> 00:17:28.600
And in some cases, willing not to listen,

00:17:28.600 --> 00:17:32.210
because founders tend to not be the
greatest listeners.

00:17:32.210 --> 00:17:35.260
And in many cases they're right because
they're doing new things,

00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:35.840
trying new things.

00:17:37.910 --> 00:17:39.870
&gt;&gt; On your on your investment philosophy,
you,

00:17:39.870 --> 00:17:44.190
you referenced this earlier that you look
for investors who come from modest means.

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:45.170
And I think you also look for

00:17:45.170 --> 00:17:48.200
entrepreneurs who come from from modest
means.

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:50.630
You said, we want people who come from
humble backgrounds, and

00:17:50.630 --> 00:17:51.320
have a need to win.

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:56.130
So what types of entrepreneurs make the
best founders?

00:17:56.130 --> 00:17:59.360
You know, wha, in particular if, say,
someone who had an MBA

00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:01.690
from the Stanford Graduate School of
Business walked into your.

00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:06.900
Into your office wh, wha, what profile do
you look for?

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:10.210
&gt;&gt; So I think entrepreneurs, like
investors,

00:18:10.210 --> 00:18:13.240
come in different flavors, and I will tell
you, and

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:18.190
as I told my kids, that if you're
desperate, it's a great asset.

00:18:18.190 --> 00:18:20.589
If you have too many choices in life.

00:18:21.650 --> 00:18:23.080
It clouds your thinking.

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:28.320
When you only have one way to go and
that's forward, it's very easy.

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:29.520
You just go, go, go.

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:32.160
Failure truly's not an option.

00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:37.410
Now, what we look for in entrepreneurs is
people that have not followed the,

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:39.580
the same tracks.

00:18:39.580 --> 00:18:43.020
People that, that have done quirky things.

00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:43.946
That have taken risks.

00:18:43.946 --> 00:18:50.170
We look for that we look for people that
have knowledge in the domain.

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:54.650
Not so much the best of minded of
business,

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:56.100
because they wanna make some money.

00:18:56.100 --> 00:18:59.640
We actually look for people that are very
interested.

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:03.900
And their product or service being used by
the next ten, 20 million people.

00:19:05.420 --> 00:19:06.410
And we also look for

00:19:06.410 --> 00:19:11.280
people their little secret that solve a
problem that they have.

00:19:12.280 --> 00:19:13.870
And it just so happened, they don't know
it,

00:19:13.870 --> 00:19:18.010
we don't know it, but they're the proxy
for the next 20 million users.

00:19:18.010 --> 00:19:23.200
So Jan of WhatsApp understood privacy and
low cost messaging.

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:24.550
He had that need.

00:19:24.550 --> 00:19:26.750
He started WhatsApp.

00:19:26.750 --> 00:19:30.810
Well, the founders of Yahoo from Stanford
couldn't find anything on

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:35.410
the internet, so they built a search
engine.

00:19:35.410 --> 00:19:37.210
They built a yellow pages.

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:39.239
Or something as simple as Zappos.

00:19:40.270 --> 00:19:44.040
The founders helpers couldn't find a pair
of shoes, and so we looked for

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:49.680
people that l, that are trying to solve
problems.

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:54.810
That they themselves have and hopefully a
new problem.

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:58.020
And if we see that, that's a little tell
for

00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:03.680
us that we may be on to something, because
we may find our first beta site.

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:05.820
The founder for the next generation.

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:11.180
&gt;&gt; Looking at, looking at Sequoia more
broadly and

00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:16.300
one issue that impacts Sequoia and the
Valley as a whole is a gender gap.

00:20:16.300 --> 00:20:17.180
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:20:17.180 --> 00:20:20.880
&gt;&gt; That there aren't a lot of women in,
working in tech,

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:26.160
or engineering and so Sequoia itself has
no female partners.

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:27.240
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:29.410
&gt;&gt; And this is something you have spoken
about before, but

00:20:29.410 --> 00:20:31.880
it would be interesting to hear your
thought son why there aren't more women in

00:20:31.880 --> 00:20:36.090
the bell in general and Sequoia, what is
the reason for that?

00:20:36.090 --> 00:20:38.650
&gt;&gt; So look, clearly I can make a lot of
excuses,

00:20:38.650 --> 00:20:40.850
not a lot of women engineers and so on.

00:20:40.850 --> 00:20:42.032
But all we need is one or two.

00:20:42.032 --> 00:20:44.359
[COUGH] So I think that's just an excuse.

00:20:44.359 --> 00:20:47.870
And I will tell you that our inability in
the U.S we

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:52.090
have women investors in India and China.

00:20:52.090 --> 00:20:54.980
Our inability to have [COUGH] women
investors at

00:20:54.980 --> 00:20:57.900
Sequoia U.S is an abject failure on our
part.

00:20:57.900 --> 00:20:59.670
Let me be crystal clear.

00:20:59.670 --> 00:21:01.620
We have a heterogeneous.

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:02.930
Set of talents at Sequoia.

00:21:02.930 --> 00:21:08.628
We have engineers, sales, marketing, CFO,

00:21:08.628 --> 00:21:12.490
Italians, Chinese, Indian.

00:21:12.490 --> 00:21:14.210
But we don't have the woman's point of
view.

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:17.890
And if you just think about consumer
internet investing,

00:21:17.890 --> 00:21:21.250
half of the population is women.

00:21:21.250 --> 00:21:25.710
So it is in our interests, both as good
citizens and as selfish foun, and

00:21:25.710 --> 00:21:29.830
as selfish investors to have that point of
view.

00:21:31.180 --> 00:21:38.120
We're, we have been looking and we will
continue to look for women investors.

00:21:38.120 --> 00:21:40.900
We actually hired someone from the GSB a
couple of years ago,

00:21:40.900 --> 00:21:45.420
spent the summer with us, and then she
decided to become an operating person.

00:21:45.420 --> 00:21:49.160
Now, yes but we do have some women
partners.

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:52.540
Blair our marketing partner, came from
Stanford.

00:21:52.540 --> 00:21:56.560
Our global CFO is a woman, but you're
absolutely right,

00:21:56.560 --> 00:22:00.570
we do not have a woman investor partner
and

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:04.329
that is a huge failure on our part and by
the way we're looking.

00:22:06.180 --> 00:22:06.910
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] All right, well.

00:22:06.910 --> 00:22:11.282
Maybe you'll get some resumes this
afternoon.

00:22:11.282 --> 00:22:13.506
So I, I wanna take us to the,

00:22:13.506 --> 00:22:17.955
a bigger picture discussion here, about
the Valley as a whole.

00:22:17.955 --> 00:22:19.710
Maybe then we could talk about Sequoia all
day.

00:22:19.710 --> 00:22:24.420
You know, times in the Valley are,
obviously, very good.

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:28.030
Sequoia had a notable success of WhatsApp.

00:22:28.030 --> 00:22:32.510
But there are a number of big acquisition
going on right now.

00:22:32.510 --> 00:22:34.370
The WhatsApp acquisition at 19 billion
makes it

00:22:34.370 --> 00:22:36.960
worth more than the following companies.

00:22:36.960 --> 00:22:41.310
Southwest Airlines, which flew me across
the country last month.

00:22:41.310 --> 00:22:45.720
Ralph Lauren, ConAgra Foods, which employs
26,000 people.

00:22:45.720 --> 00:22:49.533
And even Chipotle which feeds me three to
four times a week.

00:22:49.533 --> 00:22:52.005
[LAUGH] The list goes on.

00:22:52.005 --> 00:22:53.600
Harley-Davidson, I have a whole list here.

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:55.430
You know, you know this.

00:22:55.430 --> 00:22:59.790
And, and, I'm not questioning the value to
Facebook.

00:22:59.790 --> 00:23:03.230
What I am questioning is whether these
companies are really worth that much.

00:23:03.230 --> 00:23:06.690
Peter [UNKNOWN] has a quote that says, we
wanted flying cars and

00:23:06.690 --> 00:23:09.420
instead we got 140 characters.

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:10.680
I'm wondering how you think about that.

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:12.780
Is this an overvalued bubble?

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:15.690
Are these companies really creating the
type of value that

00:23:15.690 --> 00:23:17.370
they're being estimated at?

00:23:17.370 --> 00:23:18.480
&gt;&gt; Boy that's a lot of questions.

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:19.130
&gt;&gt; Yeah well.
&gt;&gt; You asked me

00:23:19.130 --> 00:23:20.700
if What'sApp is worth 19 billion.

00:23:20.700 --> 00:23:21.530
&gt;&gt; Well no right.

00:23:21.530 --> 00:23:23.480
&gt;&gt; You've asked me is there a bubble.

00:23:24.540 --> 00:23:26.490
So a few answers.

00:23:26.490 --> 00:23:28.510
Let's start with What'sApp.

00:23:28.510 --> 00:23:32.870
Let me ask all of you, if you owned 100%
of Facebook, and

00:23:32.870 --> 00:23:39.090
you have 190 billion market count, and you
have a huge

00:23:39.090 --> 00:23:45.360
unprotected flank called Mobile, what is
it worth to you,

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:50.810
to shore up that plan, that flank, is it
worth 10% of your market cap or not?

00:23:50.810 --> 00:23:53.450
It wasn't just What'sApp, it was Instagram
as well.

00:23:53.450 --> 00:23:54.870
That, that we sold them.

00:23:54.870 --> 00:23:57.330
With those two properties,

00:23:57.330 --> 00:24:02.500
no one talks about Facebook any longer
being weak and mobile.

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:07.800
So the value of property is in the eyes of
the buyer and the seller.

00:24:07.800 --> 00:24:10.400
I can tell you from the eyes of the buyer.

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:15.817
It was definitely worth ten percent of
their market cap to shore up

00:24:15.817 --> 00:24:17.840
that plan, that flank.

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:19.746
Next.
Whether we live in a bubble.

00:24:19.746 --> 00:24:24.394
In I think the public markets are rational
and

00:24:24.394 --> 00:24:30.508
reflect the transformation caused across U
in this, the,

00:24:30.508 --> 00:24:39.890
the industries the US, and healthcare,
manufacturing, so on, by technology.

00:24:39.890 --> 00:24:43.410
As we're seeing the number of new Fortune
1000s,

00:24:45.380 --> 00:24:50.060
the percentage of new Fortune 1000s every
decade increasing and increasing.

00:24:50.060 --> 00:24:53.170
40 years ago 30% of the Fortune 1000
changed.

00:24:53.170 --> 00:24:54.740
The last decade, 70%.

00:24:54.740 --> 00:24:56.550
So think of that rate of change.

00:24:56.550 --> 00:24:58.460
And you're, and

00:24:58.460 --> 00:25:03.260
you're seeing it reflected in what some of
these technology companies mean value.

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:06.250
Things are nutty in a private market.

00:25:06.250 --> 00:25:11.580
And the one rule, that's the most
fundamental rule of investing,

00:25:11.580 --> 00:25:13.390
is that we live in cycles.

00:25:13.390 --> 00:25:15.120
And every time you hear it's different for

00:25:15.120 --> 00:25:20.130
this time, it's the first time this is
gonna happen, that's all crap.

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:21.340
We live in cycles.

00:25:21.340 --> 00:25:26.630
And the more time you hear, the, the, the
words billion dollar market cap,

00:25:26.630 --> 00:25:31.810
the more you know you're approaching a top
of the cycle.

00:25:31.810 --> 00:25:35.110
In 1999 there were a lot of companies with
no business model, zero.

00:25:35.110 --> 00:25:40.420
What we're seeing now is a whole bunch of
companies that deserve to have, private

00:25:40.420 --> 00:25:46.110
companies that deserve to have wonderful
market value, and a whole bunch of others.

00:25:46.110 --> 00:25:51.300
That if I hid the name of the company and
I showed you the financials, you would not

00:25:51.300 --> 00:25:55.130
ascribe the value that the private markets
are ascribing to these kinds of companies.

00:25:56.240 --> 00:26:00.230
Which is a long way to saying that as long
as you have that,

00:26:00.230 --> 00:26:02.980
as soon as you have a hiccup in a public
market.

00:26:02.980 --> 00:26:04.600
The hedge funds are gonna go away.

00:26:04.600 --> 00:26:06.340
The latest stage money gonna go away.

00:26:06.340 --> 00:26:08.340
The billion dollar funding's gonna go
away.

00:26:08.340 --> 00:26:12.230
And a whole bunch of companies with
horrific business model.

00:26:12.230 --> 00:26:13.890
Maybe revenues, but

00:26:13.890 --> 00:26:17.680
terrible unit economics are not gonna be
able to raise money.

00:26:17.680 --> 00:26:20.480
And that's when it's, and then it's gonna
turn ugly real quickly.

00:26:20.480 --> 00:26:22.760
I think things in a private market, for

00:26:22.760 --> 00:26:24.970
a large number of companies, are in Lala
Land.

00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:29.150
Because the companies are not worth
anywhere,

00:26:29.150 --> 00:26:31.440
near what the private market is describing
to them.

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:33.550
Now why is all this occurring?

00:26:33.550 --> 00:26:35.970
Because first you have the investors and

00:26:35.970 --> 00:26:39.380
then you have the tourists that say oh my
god we're missing out.

00:26:40.500 --> 00:26:45.230
And so you have more and more money coming
in that's investing at higher and

00:26:45.230 --> 00:26:47.799
higher level of risks, but they don't
realize that's happening.

00:26:48.830 --> 00:26:51.850
And then something happens like, at this
location in the public market,

00:26:51.850 --> 00:26:55.700
an adjustment 500 point correction, all
the money tends to disappear, and

00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:59.280
all these companies that are used to
burning 50 billion cash a quarter in

00:26:59.280 --> 00:27:01.720
the private markets, are gonna be hurting.

00:27:01.720 --> 00:27:04.370
That is what a forecast is gonna have.

00:27:04.370 --> 00:27:06.970
&gt;&gt; So we are in somewhat of a bubble, you
say?

00:27:06.970 --> 00:27:10.970
&gt;&gt; We are, we're approaching the top of
the market you know

00:27:10.970 --> 00:27:12.362
in private market investing.

00:27:12.362 --> 00:27:20.040
&gt;&gt; Okay, [LAUGH]
so on the on the funding landscape.

00:27:20.040 --> 00:27:25.120
You we, we spoke back stage a bit about
some of the confusion that is out there

00:27:25.120 --> 00:27:28.000
there's, there seems to be in the shift in
the funding landscape.

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:33.220
People are talking about Angels and SIIDE
funders you know increasing in power and

00:27:33.220 --> 00:27:34.340
dis intermediate vcs.

00:27:35.550 --> 00:27:39.990
What's your take on the, is it a trend, is
it a real trend, what's you take on it?

00:27:39.990 --> 00:27:43.170
&gt;&gt; So look it's quite simple.

00:27:43.170 --> 00:27:46.000
With the advent of internet and mobile.

00:27:47.500 --> 00:27:51.813
We saw the birth of what I call
application, layer, and dusting.

00:27:51.813 --> 00:27:56.899
Where, now you can buy a computer for
$1,000 or less, or store your files on

00:27:56.899 --> 00:28:02.006
Amazon, or use open source, and you can
create a product for $200,000.

00:28:02.006 --> 00:28:05.730
So the cost to start one of these
companies is a lot less.

00:28:05.730 --> 00:28:07.990
Remember, it's the cost to start, not the
cost to launch.

00:28:09.040 --> 00:28:13.450
And so for the first time we've seen the
growth of Angel investing,

00:28:13.450 --> 00:28:16.580
which is necessary, because there's so
many founders and entrepreneurs.

00:28:16.580 --> 00:28:18.810
We could not make all the investments that
we see.

00:28:20.270 --> 00:28:24.320
But at the same time, we've also seen
these things called party rounds,

00:28:24.320 --> 00:28:28.410
where instead of raising $250,000, or 500,
or a million.

00:28:28.410 --> 00:28:30.410
Money's being thrown at these founders,
and

00:28:30.410 --> 00:28:33.950
they're raising these four, $5 million
rounds before they have a product.

00:28:35.040 --> 00:28:36.590
Without calculating what that means,

00:28:37.860 --> 00:28:42.440
keep in mind the Angels spend this much
time with a founder in a company's life.

00:28:42.440 --> 00:28:46.640
If a company's life is that long, the
Angels spend that much.

00:28:46.640 --> 00:28:49.380
And as a founder you have to figure out
how much equity do

00:28:49.380 --> 00:28:54.970
you wanna sell to a asset class, that
category of partners.

00:28:54.970 --> 00:28:59.340
Think of them as partners that will spend
maybe the first nine months with you.

00:28:59.340 --> 00:29:02.180
Then the venture guys come to spend the
next seven years, and you have to

00:29:02.180 --> 00:29:06.640
figure out, how much equity do you want to
sell to a partner that's next seven years.

00:29:06.640 --> 00:29:08.980
Then the public market will do the rest.

00:29:08.980 --> 00:29:12.880
So in my mind, the Angels are necessary,
especially for

00:29:12.880 --> 00:29:15.100
these application level company.

00:29:15.100 --> 00:29:18.270
If you're gonna go in the lab and build IP
for 18 months before you come out

00:29:18.270 --> 00:29:21.300
with a product, then Angel's not gonna do
much for you.

00:29:21.300 --> 00:29:23.160
But in these internet mobile companies,

00:29:23.160 --> 00:29:29.600
the Angel play a productive role as long
as you don't sell a ton of your equity.

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:33.010
And after the first round you've sold 62%
of your company.

00:29:33.010 --> 00:29:33.570
It's just nuts!

00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:37.245
You oughta be totally selfish with the
early shares,

00:29:37.245 --> 00:29:40.356
including the shares with the venture
guys.

00:29:40.356 --> 00:29:44.443
Raise as little money as you can to get
you to the next milestone,

00:29:44.443 --> 00:29:49.440
the value goes up and then when you have
got market power and you have got three,

00:29:49.440 --> 00:29:55.266
four, five, ten, $15 billion value, Uber,
18 billion or in our case, on Airbnb,

00:29:55.266 --> 00:30:00.753
ten, 12 billion, then go raise a lot of
money, but you should guard those shares-

00:30:00.753 --> 00:30:05.208
With your life, and you should architect
your investors the same way you

00:30:05.208 --> 00:30:08.430
architect your product and your
engineering team.

00:30:09.680 --> 00:30:12.040
&gt;&gt; How should founders think about raising
money then?

00:30:12.040 --> 00:30:14.490
Should they, you know if you wanna protect
their equity but

00:30:14.490 --> 00:30:16.210
they wanna raise money as fast as
possible.

00:30:16.210 --> 00:30:20.140
&gt;&gt; Raise as little as you can to get you
to something that you can show,

00:30:20.140 --> 00:30:23.130
plus maybe a quarter or two, so you have a
little bit of cushion.

00:30:23.130 --> 00:30:25.110
And then raise some more money.

00:30:25.110 --> 00:30:29.121
Raise as little, not as much, as little as
you can.

00:30:29.121 --> 00:30:32.100
Because that's the most expensive equity
you're gonna sell.

00:30:33.370 --> 00:30:34.398
&gt;&gt; Raise as little-
&gt;&gt; And, and

00:30:34.398 --> 00:30:39.967
conversely be very generous with the early
engineers that you hire.

00:30:39.967 --> 00:30:41.270
Those are the ones you should invest in.

00:30:42.730 --> 00:30:47.035
Because the first two or three engineers,
if you get those wrong, you are done.

00:30:47.035 --> 00:30:51.246
[LAUGH].

00:30:51.246 --> 00:30:52.964
&gt;&gt; So we all should become engineers is
what you're saying?

00:30:52.964 --> 00:30:53.522
[LAUGH].

00:30:53.522 --> 00:30:56.431
&gt;&gt; No, no, because if you're building a
technical product,

00:30:56.431 --> 00:30:59.420
an A plus engineer is gonna help you to
recruit an A engineer.

00:30:59.420 --> 00:31:04.046
If your first two or three engineers are B
engineers, you're done, because you'll

00:31:04.046 --> 00:31:08.624
never surround yourself wi, with the, you
know, with the A plus talented people.

00:31:08.624 --> 00:31:10.590
&gt;&gt; Got it.
Well it's good to be at Stanford then.

00:31:10.590 --> 00:31:12.520
We got some good engineers here.

00:31:12.520 --> 00:31:16.350
We only have time for about one more
question before we move to the audience,

00:31:16.350 --> 00:31:20.260
and I just wanted to touch on Sequoia's
success in emerging markets.

00:31:20.260 --> 00:31:20.910
You know, you have,

00:31:20.910 --> 00:31:26.470
have found success in Israel, India, and
particularly China where others have not.

00:31:26.470 --> 00:31:30.590
I'm wondering, you know, is this just
another secret sauce kinda thing?

00:31:30.590 --> 00:31:31.780
You hire the best people and

00:31:31.780 --> 00:31:34.470
they do good things or what, what has led
to success there?

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:38.792
&gt;&gt; A little luck, we found good people.

00:31:38.792 --> 00:31:42.401
but, but we looked for a while.

00:31:42.401 --> 00:31:47.704
And I think the courage and foolishness,
because I think in retrospect with,

00:31:47.704 --> 00:31:52.117
I mean, that we can believe that we did
this, is to hire people and

00:31:52.117 --> 00:31:54.445
work the attack side of the house,

00:31:54.445 --> 00:32:00.349
which is the money making, the investing,
the hiring, to have that decentralized.

00:32:00.349 --> 00:32:04.300
To not have every decision go through
California, because what do I

00:32:04.300 --> 00:32:08.686
know about what goes on in China, what do
I know about what goes on in India?

00:32:08.686 --> 00:32:11.493
I traveled to India and China four to five
times a year, but

00:32:11.493 --> 00:32:14.491
at the end of the day, I don't really know
what goes on there.

00:32:14.491 --> 00:32:20.250
So we decentralize the attacks out of the
house, and we centralize the defense.

00:32:20.250 --> 00:32:22.920
The stay out of jail compliance.

00:32:22.920 --> 00:32:24.520
The financial reporting.

00:32:24.520 --> 00:32:28.900
No one has had the courage to do what we
did.

00:32:28.900 --> 00:32:35.280
To completely decentralize and have
relationships and

00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:40.630
trust be the things that really hold us as
one firm.

00:32:40.630 --> 00:32:43.330
It's not a franchise opportunity, we are
one partnership.

00:32:43.330 --> 00:32:47.430
It's not Sequoia Capital China, it's
Sequoia Capital in China,

00:32:47.430 --> 00:32:54.140
to show you how carefully we even use our
words.

00:32:54.140 --> 00:32:55.520
And essentially we are,

00:32:55.520 --> 00:33:00.290
we're held by a whole bunch of strings,
compensation is a small piece of it.

00:33:00.290 --> 00:33:01.590
A lot of it is culture.

00:33:01.590 --> 00:33:06.810
When we send email to a partner in China,
we get a response right back the same way

00:33:06.810 --> 00:33:10.130
that when I send an email to my partner
here in the US.

00:33:10.130 --> 00:33:12.770
And we view our partners in China and
India and

00:33:12.770 --> 00:33:15.130
Israel the same way as the partners in the
US.

00:33:15.130 --> 00:33:19.802
So having that patience, that courage,
that foolishness to organize that when

00:33:19.802 --> 00:33:24.300
investing all the time, I think has been
the secret to our success.

00:33:24.300 --> 00:33:25.323
&gt;&gt; Interesting.

00:33:25.323 --> 00:33:28.210
Well I wanna save some time for questions
here.

00:33:28.210 --> 00:33:32.810
We have mics in the front rows here, so
please start raising your hands.

00:33:32.810 --> 00:33:35.380
If you're in the top deck you can tweet
questions and

00:33:35.380 --> 00:33:37.620
we'll have someone read a few of those.

00:33:37.620 --> 00:33:40.786
And we have time for about ten, ten -15
minutes of questions.

00:33:40.786 --> 00:33:42.016
So.

00:33:42.016 --> 00:33:43.660
We got one right.

00:33:43.660 --> 00:33:44.780
&gt;&gt; Right in front.

00:33:44.780 --> 00:33:47.170
We'll go right over here.

00:33:47.170 --> 00:33:49.632
And please stand up and introduce
yourself.

00:33:49.632 --> 00:33:51.250
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name is-

00:33:51.250 --> 00:33:52.700
&gt;&gt; Yep, just hold it close.

00:33:52.700 --> 00:33:56.560
&gt;&gt; My name is Federico Anthony part of the
faculty here at Stanford GSB.

00:33:56.560 --> 00:33:59.392
I have a question regarding Brazil.

00:33:59.392 --> 00:34:03.844
So from the, the exterior it seems that
you didn't have as

00:34:03.844 --> 00:34:08.170
much success in Brazil as you did in China
and India.

00:34:08.170 --> 00:34:10.480
I don't know if you could speak to the
reasons for that and

00:34:10.480 --> 00:34:13.360
what learning did you get from that
experience?

00:34:13.360 --> 00:34:19.900
&gt;&gt; So, we hired about three years ago a
terrific GSB graduate.

00:34:19.900 --> 00:34:25.010
We hired him in October his first year to
work with us through year one,

00:34:25.010 --> 00:34:27.470
through the summer, and through year two.

00:34:27.470 --> 00:34:30.260
And we had a notion that we were gonna go
to Brazil.

00:34:30.260 --> 00:34:35.470
And we traveled to Brazil probably six,
seven times.

00:34:35.470 --> 00:34:36.850
We made two investments.

00:34:38.870 --> 00:34:43.330
And the more we traveled down there, the,
the more it became apparent

00:34:44.540 --> 00:34:47.410
that there were very few engineers coming
out of Brazil.

00:34:48.880 --> 00:34:50.860
And we made and

00:34:50.860 --> 00:34:55.820
we understood that we would have to go
into other lines of business.

00:34:55.820 --> 00:35:00.450
Like the the consumer industry,
supermarkets, restaurants.

00:35:00.450 --> 00:35:06.360
And we want Sequoia Capital to really be a
IT first partnership.

00:35:06.360 --> 00:35:09.100
What we didn't wanna do, is go to Brazil
and

00:35:09.100 --> 00:35:11.060
back a whole bunch of look-alike
companies.

00:35:12.100 --> 00:35:17.280
So, we hired a gentleman, he opened an
office,

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:19.330
we've made two investments, and then we
pulled back.

00:35:20.350 --> 00:35:23.270
Now there is a second part to that story.

00:35:23.270 --> 00:35:25.410
So, we did the right thing.

00:35:25.410 --> 00:35:27.950
At Sequoia, we pride ourselves at doing
the right thing.

00:35:27.950 --> 00:35:29.130
A young person.

00:35:29.130 --> 00:35:32.560
We full invested in a fund, including the
what's up funds.

00:35:32.560 --> 00:35:34.180
So he's very happy.

00:35:34.180 --> 00:35:36.260
And he wanted to start a company, and

00:35:36.260 --> 00:35:40.080
we gave him a bit of a throwaway million
dollar seed.

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:44.450
Not throwaway because that's a belittling
term, but in a long shot startup in

00:35:44.450 --> 00:35:49.080
Brazil, and he wanted to start an online
credit card company.

00:35:49.080 --> 00:35:53.210
So we helped him, board meetings over the
phone for the first year.

00:35:53.210 --> 00:35:56.450
And then at a million dollars, he
launched.

00:35:56.450 --> 00:36:00.290
A credit card company in Brazil, there's a
whole bunch,

00:36:00.290 --> 00:36:03.220
there's a line out the door of users who
want to get the credit card.

00:36:04.750 --> 00:36:06.200
They changed the law in Brazil, so

00:36:06.200 --> 00:36:09.160
now if you wanna launch a competitor, it
takes three years.

00:36:09.160 --> 00:36:12.180
And we just invested $11 million on a
series A,

00:36:12.180 --> 00:36:14.530
I have to just throw away a million
dollars.

00:36:14.530 --> 00:36:17.120
Of course, we now have three investments
in Brazil, but

00:36:17.120 --> 00:36:22.280
we have no intention to planning a flag in
Brazil,

00:36:22.280 --> 00:36:26.460
mostly because of the number of computer
science engineers.

00:36:26.460 --> 00:36:31.000
The last thing I'll tell you is, I met
with a gentleman, a gardener,

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:32.880
lives in Brazil.

00:36:32.880 --> 00:36:37.930
He he does software monitoring throughout
the US, he's the one that writes all the,

00:36:37.930 --> 00:36:40.140
all the graphic software, and I asked him,

00:36:40.140 --> 00:36:42.870
what were the two three leading software
companies in Brazil?

00:36:42.870 --> 00:36:44.160
He could only name one or two.

00:36:44.160 --> 00:36:48.625
It was very clear that it was too early to
go to Brazil from an IT,

00:36:48.625 --> 00:36:51.860
non-look-alike internet company market.

00:36:51.860 --> 00:36:54.100
And so we backed off and that's the real
story.

00:36:54.100 --> 00:36:55.981
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:36:55.981 --> 00:36:57.885
&gt;&gt; All right, I think we have one more
question over here.

00:36:57.885 --> 00:37:00.161
&gt;&gt; Well, my name is Nam, Nama.

00:37:00.161 --> 00:37:03.950
I'm an MBA class of 2016.

00:37:03.950 --> 00:37:09.060
And I think by now my classmates are,
already know what I'm about to ask.

00:37:09.060 --> 00:37:13.920
We already touched a little bit about
gender inequality in your firm.

00:37:13.920 --> 00:37:19.450
I wonder how, how it affects how the firm
addresses, women entrepreneurship.

00:37:19.450 --> 00:37:25.822
Are you missing out on women entrepreneurs
because you don't have a female partner?

00:37:25.822 --> 00:37:31.050
&gt;&gt; Well I, I, you know, we have no way of
knowing if we're missing out.

00:37:32.830 --> 00:37:38.569
So in the last 10 years I have served in
half a dozen boards

00:37:39.610 --> 00:37:45.310
where the lead founder, CEO, is or was a
woman.

00:37:45.310 --> 00:37:48.810
So we have no fear of backing women.

00:37:48.810 --> 00:37:56.372
Right now in one of my best companies, the
president, founder, is a woman.

00:37:56.372 --> 00:37:59.710
House, another company where I'm not
aboard.

00:37:59.710 --> 00:38:03.510
The CEO is a woman, so we have no issues
in backing women.

00:38:03.510 --> 00:38:08.540
I think we're missing out as investors
mostly because as, as I said earlier,

00:38:08.540 --> 00:38:12.090
we're missing a point of view that
represents half of the population.

00:38:12.090 --> 00:38:14.660
And if you do any studies on high
performance team,

00:38:14.660 --> 00:38:17.840
high performance team have heterogeneous
point of view, and

00:38:17.840 --> 00:38:21.560
we're missing half of the population of
the U.S..

00:38:21.560 --> 00:38:24.760
But we, we have been in business with
numerous women and

00:38:24.760 --> 00:38:27.640
we look forward to being in business with
even more women.

00:38:27.640 --> 00:38:32.531
So if you wanna start a company, come to
Sequoia we'd love to hear your pitch.

00:38:32.531 --> 00:38:33.964
&gt;&gt; There we go.

00:38:33.964 --> 00:38:38.854
We're gonna take one from Twitter, but
please keep your hands raised so

00:38:38.854 --> 00:38:43.676
they can give you the mic, but we'll go to
a Twitter question now.

00:38:43.676 --> 00:38:45.290
The Twitter question.

00:38:45.290 --> 00:38:46.161
&gt;&gt; I'm not sure [UNKNOWN].

00:38:46.161 --> 00:38:47.762
&gt;&gt; Have they, great, thank you.

00:38:47.762 --> 00:38:52.430
The Twitter question I wanna ask is, you
contribute a lot of your success to luck.

00:38:52.430 --> 00:38:56.505
Do you have any habits or ways of
increasing your chance of being lucky?

00:38:56.505 --> 00:39:01.250
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
Yeah I think I do and

00:39:01.250 --> 00:39:06.740
my partners sometimes get upset at me.

00:39:06.740 --> 00:39:11.260
I lean forward, not in writing checks but
in listening.

00:39:11.260 --> 00:39:16.370
So I remember being on a panel with
another venture person who said,

00:39:16.370 --> 00:39:21.580
I won't tell you his name, he said, well,
we like our business plan to

00:39:21.580 --> 00:39:25.502
come from screen sources, lawyers and so
on.

00:39:25.502 --> 00:39:29.401
And then, and then it was my turn to
answer the question,

00:39:29.401 --> 00:39:35.771
this is a little before email, I said
854-3927, meaning our phone number.

00:39:35.771 --> 00:39:42.986
And so I, I the thing I like to do, I like
to go look in all the nooks and

00:39:42.986 --> 00:39:48.678
cranny's, where it's not hip to go look.

00:39:48.678 --> 00:39:54.691
Or when I hear something something's
really crappy,

00:39:54.691 --> 00:39:57.710
the this is really a bad time.

00:39:58.920 --> 00:40:03.510
My brain works, it's about time, terrific,
I wanna go look.

00:40:04.560 --> 00:40:08.010
And then just it's from my sales days.

00:40:08.010 --> 00:40:10.580
I, I guess now if you're in sales they
hand you leads.

00:40:11.590 --> 00:40:14.050
When I was in sales, nobody ever handed me
a lead.

00:40:14.050 --> 00:40:16.130
It was lot of cold calling.

00:40:16.130 --> 00:40:20.870
And in one company, especially the company
where the lady's the president,

00:40:20.870 --> 00:40:25.160
I am terrifically proud that I co-called
it myself.

00:40:25.160 --> 00:40:26.440
Is that luck?

00:40:26.440 --> 00:40:29.130
No, I think hustle, luck, and clearly lo,
look.

00:40:29.130 --> 00:40:33.280
There's a lot of people as talented as I,
that could be sitting right here.

00:40:33.280 --> 00:40:35.950
I don't have any special talents, I wanna
make that crystal clear.

00:40:35.950 --> 00:40:37.700
There's a lot of people that could be
sitting over here and yet

00:40:37.700 --> 00:40:40.080
I'm the one sitting here so luck has
something to do with it.

00:40:40.080 --> 00:40:44.340
But I will tell you, hustle has a great
deal to do with it as well.

00:40:46.566 --> 00:40:48.606
&gt;&gt; I think we have a question on this
side.

00:40:48.606 --> 00:40:49.117
&gt;&gt; Hi Doug.

00:40:49.117 --> 00:40:50.116
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:40:50.116 --> 00:40:51.462
You, you can just talk into it.

00:40:51.462 --> 00:40:52.646
It, it's turned on.

00:40:52.646 --> 00:40:53.273
Yep.

00:40:53.273 --> 00:40:56.110
&gt;&gt; Hi Doug, my name is Yeme, I'm from
Qualcomm.

00:40:56.110 --> 00:40:58.580
And you said you are seizing in cycles.

00:40:58.580 --> 00:41:02.280
Like as you mentioned the mobile
applications we're approaching.

00:41:03.340 --> 00:41:09.250
And can you please share with us in your
mind what's the next things.

00:41:09.250 --> 00:41:14.160
The cycle is about to start or maybe next
several things you can see that.

00:41:14.160 --> 00:41:15.120
Thank you.

00:41:15.120 --> 00:41:16.630
&gt;&gt; So it, look.

00:41:16.630 --> 00:41:18.760
People always ask me what's hot.

00:41:18.760 --> 00:41:20.650
And here's my answer.

00:41:20.650 --> 00:41:24.765
If I could tell you by definition, it's
not hot.

00:41:24.765 --> 00:41:28.970
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The, the day before I came

00:41:28.970 --> 00:41:32.680
across Google I would not have told you,

00:41:32.680 --> 00:41:38.790
a new market entrant in search, there were
20 search company, is hot.

00:41:38.790 --> 00:41:42.770
Or the day before YouTube when, when video
and

00:41:42.770 --> 00:41:46.650
that had failed consistently, I would have
never told you.

00:41:46.650 --> 00:41:47.150
That's hot.

00:41:48.370 --> 00:41:54.260
We have market maps, but market maps by
definition look at the status quo and

00:41:54.260 --> 00:41:58.650
extend the line, and once in a while we
find the company that fills the hole.

00:41:58.650 --> 00:42:03.380
But the best investments we make are the
things we haven't even thought of.

00:42:03.380 --> 00:42:05.360
Imagine Air B and B.

00:42:06.500 --> 00:42:10.330
Imagine you asking me that question the
day before NBNB and

00:42:10.330 --> 00:42:12.660
I'm gonna tell you" You know I'll tell you
what's odd.

00:42:12.660 --> 00:42:16.850
some guy is gonna (There were three guys
in that case.) three guys are gonna show

00:42:16.850 --> 00:42:21.670
up and they're gonna tell me that that's
how it is a situation where I'm gonna rent

00:42:21.670 --> 00:42:24.790
rooms in my house to strangers.

00:42:24.790 --> 00:42:28.460
We're gonna create a global two sided
market place in that.

00:42:28.460 --> 00:42:31.170
And I want to say come on, that's nuts.

00:42:31.170 --> 00:42:36.410
So, that is a question that by definition
you cannot answer.

00:42:36.410 --> 00:42:39.455
Now I will tell you, than in the last
fund.

00:42:39.455 --> 00:42:42.640
[UNKNOWN] Capital, it's a three year fund.

00:42:42.640 --> 00:42:47.940
We have fewer consumer internet mobile
companies than we had in the fund before.

00:42:47.940 --> 00:42:50.730
I can't tell you if it's because we're
missing something or

00:42:50.730 --> 00:42:53.490
